# pubsub-ingestion.yaml.example - Pub/Sub push ingestion pattern for atomic-agents
#
# NOT YET DOGFOODED against the live GCP platform.
# Claims verified against GCP documentation as of 2026-06-17.
# See extras/gcp/README.md §External-claim verification status.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS:
# This file shows gcloud CLI commands to provision Pub/Sub resources (topic +
# dead-letter topic + DLQ pull subscription + push subscription). It is NOT a
# declarative Terraform/API resource file. Pub/Sub has no equivalent of Cloud
# Scheduler's YAML export format. The field names in comments reflect the
# Pub/Sub REST API Subscription resource schema (camelCase), verified against
# https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/reference/rest/v1/projects.subscriptions.
# The gcloud flag names differ; they are shown in the actual commands below.
#
# ─── TWO-HOP TOPOLOGY: PUSH → TRANSFORM → SERVE ──────────────────────────────
#
# Pub/Sub push CANNOT target the /call endpoint directly: push delivers a
# base64 JSON ENVELOPE, and the Idempotency-Key must become an HTTP HEADER —
# neither of which a raw push can do. So the push subscription's endpoint is the
# pubsub-to-call-transform Cloud Function (TRANSFORM_URL), and the transform
# forwards a flat body + Idempotency-Key header to Cloud Run /call. Two OIDC
# hops result:
#   HOP 1: Pub/Sub push SA → invokes the Cloud Function   (audience = TRANSFORM_URL)
#   HOP 2: transform's own SA → invokes Cloud Run /call    (audience = CLOUD_RUN_URL)
# The IAM grants below cover HOP 1. HOP 2 is handled inside the transform via
# fetch_id_token(CLOUD_RUN_SERVICE_URL) and the transform SA's run.invoker grant
# (see pubsub-to-call-transform.py.example deployment notes).
#
# ─── OIDC PUSH AUTH — REQUIRED IAM GRANTS ────────────────────────────────────
#
# Pub/Sub push OIDC auth requires BOTH of the following grants, not just one:
#
# GRANT 1: Allow the push SA to invoke the Cloud FUNCTION (the push endpoint).
# The transform is a Cloud Functions GEN2 function. The Pub/Sub push-auth doc of
# record prescribes roles/cloudfunctions.invoker for the push SA when push is
# used as an HTTP trigger to a function, and the function-level invoker command
# is the surface to use — it does NOT require you to know the function's backing
# Cloud Run service name:
#
#   gcloud functions add-invoker-policy-binding <your-transform-function> \
#     --member=serviceAccount:PUSH_SA@PROJECT.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#     --region=REGION
#
# (Verified: the push-auth doc states "Your push auth service account must have
#  the roles/cloudfunctions.invoker role to invoke a function if you intend to
#  use Pub/Sub push requests as HTTP triggers to the function" —
#  https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/authenticate-push-subscriptions . The
#  add-invoker-policy-binding command "adds the Cloud Run Invoker binding to the
#  IAM policy of the specified function's underlying Cloud Run service" for Gen2 —
#  https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/functions/add-invoker-policy-binding )
#
# EQUIVALENT LOWER-LEVEL ALTERNATIVE (Gen2 only): because a Gen2 function is
# backed by a Cloud Run service, granting roles/run.invoker directly on that
# backing service achieves the same invoke permission. The function-level command
# above is preferred (it is the push-doc-of-record surface and is generation-
# agnostic); use the run.invoker form only if you specifically need to bind the
# backing service directly, and confirm at deploy time:
#
#   gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding <transform-backing-run-service> \
#     --member=serviceAccount:PUSH_SA@PROJECT.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#     --role=roles/run.invoker \
#     --region=REGION
#   # (Gen1 functions would instead use roles/cloudfunctions.invoker on the
#   #  function resource; this reference uses Gen2.)
#
# GRANT 2: Allow the Pub/Sub service agent to mint OIDC tokens on the push SA's
# behalf. Without this, Pub/Sub cannot generate the OIDC token for push delivery
# and every push message fails immediately — all messages go to DLQ.
#
#   gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding PROJECT_ID \
#     --member=serviceAccount:service-PROJECT_NUMBER@gcp-sa-pubsub.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#     --role=roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator
#
# To find your project number:
#   gcloud projects describe PROJECT_ID --format="value(projectNumber)"
#
# Note: unlike Cloud Scheduler (which uses a simpler token path), Pub/Sub push
# authentication has an extra hop through the Pub/Sub service agent to mint OIDC
# tokens. Both grants are load-bearing. If you only do Grant 1 and see 403s,
# check whether Grant 2 is also applied.
# (Verified at: https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/authenticate-push-subscriptions)
#
# GRANT 3 (DLQ): The Pub/Sub service agent also needs publisher rights on the
# dead-letter topic so it can forward dead-lettered messages there:
#
#   gcloud pubsub topics add-iam-policy-binding atomic-agents-deadletter \
#     --member=serviceAccount:service-PROJECT_NUMBER@gcp-sa-pubsub.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#     --role=roles/pubsub.publisher
#
# GRANT 4 (DLQ): The Pub/Sub service agent needs subscriber rights on the source
# subscription so it can forward messages:
#
#   gcloud pubsub subscriptions add-iam-policy-binding atomic-agents-sub \
#     --member=serviceAccount:service-PROJECT_NUMBER@gcp-sa-pubsub.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#     --role=roles/pubsub.subscriber
#
# (Grants 3 and 4 verified at:
#  https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/handling-failures#grant_forwarding_roles)
#
# ─── OIDC AUDIENCE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The push subscription's OIDC token audience MUST match the PUSH ENDPOINT — and
# the push endpoint is the Cloud Function transform (TRANSFORM_URL), NOT the
# Cloud Run /call URL. A mismatch causes HTTP 401 on every push delivery — all
# messages go to DLQ. Copy the function URL from the Cloud Functions console.
# No trailing slash. Same rule as Cloud Scheduler (see
# cloudscheduler-jobs.yaml.example §INGRESS RECONCILIATION).
# (The CLOUD_RUN_URL audience is used by the SECOND hop, inside the transform,
# not by the push subscription.)
# For topology (a) same-project + direct function URL: audience = TRANSFORM_URL.
# For topology (b) via LB/IAP fronting the function: audience = the ALB/custom domain URL.
#
# ─── INGRESS RECONCILIATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# cloudrun-service.yaml sets run.googleapis.com/ingress =
# internal-and-cloud-load-balancing, which admits same-project internal sources
# AND traffic via an external ALB. The GCP internal-traffic source list (Cloud
# Scheduler, Pub/Sub, Eventarc, ...) is defined under `internal` and inherited
# under internal-and-cloud-load-balancing.
#
# Doc-literal: same-project Pub/Sub push to the default run.app URL is admitted
# as internal traffic. (Verified: https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/securing/ingress)
# Confirm at deploy time — same operational caveat as Cloud Scheduler.
#
# Two topology options (mirror of cloudscheduler-jobs.yaml.example):
#   (a) SAME-PROJECT + DEFAULT run.app URL:
#       Push SA OIDC token reaches Cloud Run as internal traffic.
#       Audience: the run.app service URL (no trailing slash).
#       If push returns 403/404, switch to (b) or bare `internal` ingress.
#   (b) CROSS-PROJECT or SAFE DEFAULT:
#       Route push through the external ALB / IAP.
#       Cross-project Pub/Sub does NOT qualify as same-project internal traffic;
#       must use topology (b) for any cross-project push subscription.
#       Audience: the ALB/custom domain URL.
#
# ─── AUDIT RECORD — http_caller ABSENT ON PUSH CALLS ─────────────────────────
#
# Pub/Sub push deliveries carry the push SA OIDC token in the Authorization:
# Bearer header — a header the serve layer NEVER reads. That OIDC token is
# verified by Cloud Run's own infrastructure (the perimeter), not by serve.
# Serve reads caller identity ONLY from the configured identity header
# (default X-Goog-IAP-JWT-Assertion), and per spec/37 MUST 6 it reads that
# header's value verbatim and MUST NOT attempt JWT verification of it. On a
# Pub/Sub push delivery the identity header is ABSENT (the push carries an
# Authorization bearer token, not an IAP assertion), so there is simply no
# identity for serve to record.
#
# Result: push-triggered agent.call() runs have NO http_caller field in the
# JSONL audit record. This is expected (same behavior as Cloud Scheduler calls).
#
# To distinguish push-ingestion runs from interactive runs:
#   - filter on trigger='http' combined with absence of http_caller field, OR
#   - correlate with Cloud Logging Pub/Sub delivery logs.
#
# For redelivered messages, the JSONL log will show multiple records sharing
# the same idempotency_key value (when set). The first will have status='ok';
# subsequent redeliveries will have status='deduped'. Filter on
# idempotency_key to correlate all runs for a given message.
#
# ─── SINGLE-HOST DEDUP CONSTRAINT ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# FilesystemDedupLedger uses O_EXCL atomicity (single-host only; spec/45
# MUST 7 single_host_only=True). Two Cloud Run instances do not share a
# filesystem idempotency/ directory — each would see every key as FRESH,
# defeating dedup. The Cloud Run service MUST remain at max-instances=1
# (see cloudrun-service.yaml).
# Scale beyond 1 instance requires a Redis or Postgres IdempotencyBackend.
#
# ─── DEDUP LEDGER — EPHEMERAL ON CLOUD RUN ────────────────────────────────────
#
# On stateless Cloud Run, the idempotency/ directory lives in the container's
# ephemeral writable layer and is LOST on every container replacement / redeploy.
# After a container restart, all *.lease.json and *.terminal.json files are
# gone: a redelivered message that arrives after the restart will see FRESH and
# re-run, regardless of whether it previously completed.
#
# For dedup that survives restarts: use the Compute Engine VM topology
# (extras/gcp/compute-vm/, persistent disk) or a future Redis/Postgres
# IdempotencyBackend.
#
# On the VM topology, stale IN_FLIGHT leases require manual recovery after a
# crash — see extras/gcp/README.md §Troubleshooting for the command.
#
# ─── ACK DEADLINE vs AGENT RUNTIME ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# IMPORTANT: ackDeadlineSeconds MUST be greater than your expected
# agent.call() wall-clock time. Cloud Run --timeout-seconds MUST be >=
# ackDeadlineSeconds. If agent.call() takes longer than ackDeadlineSeconds:
#
#   Cloud Run returns HTTP 504 to the transform → transform NACKs (500)
#   → Pub/Sub redelivers with the same Idempotency-Key
#   → if the original run is still IN_FLIGHT, serve returns HTTP 409
#   → the transform NACKs the 409 (correct — see below) → Pub/Sub redelivers
#     again after retryPolicy backoff
#   → once the original run terminates, the next redelivery dedups (200) or
#     re-runs; a genuinely-wedged key reaches the DLQ after maxDeliveryAttempts
#
# NACKing the 409 is the SAFE behavior, not a bug: ACKing an in-flight delivery
# would drop the only surviving copy of the work if the concurrent run later
# fails/ESCALATEs (its lease releases WITHOUT committing, spec/45 W4). The
# transform deliberately NACKs 409 (pubsub-to-call-transform.py.example STEP
# status handling). Bound the redelivery pressure with retryPolicy backoff +
# a long-enough ackDeadline so 504s are rare in the first place — do NOT try to
# suppress redelivery by ACKing in-flight work.
#
# Recommended safe values: ackDeadlineSeconds=600 (maximum), Cloud Run
# --timeout=3600. Set Cloud Run timeout >= ackDeadlineSeconds to avoid this loop.
#
# ─── DLQ DRAIN — DO NOT POINT AT /call ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# The pull subscription on the DLQ topic (atomic-agents-deadletter-sub below)
# is for manual inspection and alerting — NOT for automatic re-processing.
# Do NOT wire a DLQ drain push subscription to /call unless you specifically
# understand the dedup consequences. When Pub/Sub forwards an undeliverable
# message to the dead-letter topic, it "wraps the original message in a new one
# and adds attributes that identify the source subscription"
# (https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/handling-failures) — i.e. the
# dead-lettered message is a NEW envelope. INFERRED (NOT doc-stated, 2026-06-17):
# the wrapped message is expected to carry its OWN messageId distinct from the
# original — the cited handling-failures doc enumerates only the ADDED
# CloudPubSubDeadLetterSource* attributes and does NOT explicitly state the
# wrapped message gets a new messageId; treat this as an inference from "wraps
# the original message in a new one." So a DLQ-drain push to /call with a
# messageId-derived Idempotency-Key would (under that inference) carry a NEW
# messageId and therefore RE-RUN the agent (duplicate external effects), NOT
# dedup against the original run. The original publisher's explicit Tier-1
# message.attributes.idempotency_key is EXPECTED to survive DLQ forwarding —
# INFERRED, NOT doc-stated: the handling-failures doc lists the added
# CloudPubSubDeadLetterSource* attributes but does NOT explicitly confirm
# original-attribute preservation (2026-06-17). Confirm by inspecting a
# dead-lettered message before relying on a Tier-1 key to dedup across the drain;
# if attributes were stripped on wrapping, even a Tier-1 key would re-run. Under
# the preservation assumption it is the ONLY key that dedups across the drain.
# This matches README.md §"Idempotency-Key derivation"
# (a republished/re-injected message gets a NEW messageId → re-runs if Tier 2
# is used). Document and decide this deliberately.
#
# ─── PREREQUISITE ORDERING ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Create resources in this order (earlier resources must exist before later ones):
#   1. Dead-letter topic
#   2. DLQ pull subscription (REQUIRED — without this, dead-lettered messages are
#      permanently lost AND the monitoring alert never fires)
#   3. Source topic
#   4. Push subscription (references dead-letter topic in deadLetterPolicy)
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── HOW TO RUN THESE COMMANDS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every command below is commented out (each line begins with `#`). To run one,
# remove the leading `#` from the command lines — AND delete the interspersed
# annotation lines (the `# ackDeadlineSeconds=600...`-style explanations that sit
# between a flag's trailing `\` and the next flag). Those annotation lines are
# documentation, NOT part of the command: left in place after uncommenting, a
# comment line between two `\`-continued lines breaks the backslash continuation
# in most shells. Uncomment the flag lines only.
#
# ── STEP 0: Set shared variables ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Substitute these into the commands below.
#
# PROJECT_ID=your-project-id
# PROJECT_NUMBER=your-project-number  # gcloud projects describe $PROJECT_ID --format="value(projectNumber)"
# REGION=us-central1
# AGENT_NAME=your-agent-name
# PUSH_SA=pubsub-push-sa@${PROJECT_ID}.iam.gserviceaccount.com
# CLOUD_RUN_URL=https://YOUR_CLOUD_RUN_SERVICE_URL   # no trailing slash (audience for the transform→serve hop)
# TRANSFORM_URL=https://YOUR_CLOUD_FUNCTION_URL      # the pubsub-to-call-transform Cloud Function URL; the PUSH endpoint
# TOPIC=atomic-agents-work
# DLQ_TOPIC=atomic-agents-deadletter
# DLQ_SUB=atomic-agents-deadletter-sub
# PUSH_SUB=atomic-agents-push-sub

# ── STEP 1: Create the dead-letter topic (must exist BEFORE the push subscription) ──

# gcloud pubsub topics create ${DLQ_TOPIC} --project=${PROJECT_ID}

# ── STEP 2: Create the DLQ pull subscription (REQUIRED) ───────────────────────
# This subscription is required for two reasons:
#   (1) Without a subscription on the dead-letter topic, all dead-lettered
#       messages are permanently lost — Pub/Sub discards unsubscribed messages.
#   (2) The Cloud Monitoring alert (cloud-monitoring-policies.yaml, DLQ alert)
#       measures num_undelivered_messages on THIS subscription — without it,
#       the alert never fires even when messages are dead-lettered.
#
# This is a pull subscription for manual inspection. Do NOT add a push endpoint
# here unless you explicitly want DLQ auto-retry (and understand the dedup
# implications — see §DLQ DRAIN above).
#
# gcloud pubsub subscriptions create ${DLQ_SUB} \
#   --topic=${DLQ_TOPIC} \
#   --project=${PROJECT_ID}

# ── STEP 3: Create the source topic ───────────────────────────────────────────

# gcloud pubsub topics create ${TOPIC} --project=${PROJECT_ID}

# ── STEP 4: Apply IAM grants (see §OIDC PUSH AUTH above) ─────────────────────

# Grant 1: push SA → roles/cloudfunctions.invoker on the Cloud FUNCTION transform
# (the push endpoint; the function-level command binds the Gen2 backing service).
# gcloud functions add-invoker-policy-binding <your-transform-function> \
#   --member=serviceAccount:${PUSH_SA} \
#   --region=${REGION}
# (Equivalent lower-level alternative: roles/run.invoker on the transform's
#  backing Cloud Run service — see §OIDC PUSH AUTH Grant 1 above for the tradeoff.)
#
# Grant 1b: the TRANSFORM's own SA → roles/run.invoker on the agent service
# (HOP 2; lets the transform call /call). Substitute the transform's runtime SA.
# gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding ${AGENT_NAME} \
#   --member=serviceAccount:<transform-runtime-sa>@${PROJECT_ID}.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#   --role=roles/run.invoker \
#   --region=${REGION}

# Grant 2: Pub/Sub service agent → roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator
# gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding ${PROJECT_ID} \
#   --member=serviceAccount:service-${PROJECT_NUMBER}@gcp-sa-pubsub.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#   --role=roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator

# Grant 3: Pub/Sub service agent → roles/pubsub.publisher on DLQ topic
# gcloud pubsub topics add-iam-policy-binding ${DLQ_TOPIC} \
#   --member=serviceAccount:service-${PROJECT_NUMBER}@gcp-sa-pubsub.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#   --role=roles/pubsub.publisher

# Grant 4: Pub/Sub service agent → roles/pubsub.subscriber on source subscription
# (required AFTER the push subscription is created in Step 5; apply again if needed)
# gcloud pubsub subscriptions add-iam-policy-binding ${PUSH_SUB} \
#   --member=serviceAccount:service-${PROJECT_NUMBER}@gcp-sa-pubsub.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
#   --role=roles/pubsub.subscriber

# ── STEP 5: Create the push subscription ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# Pub/Sub REST API Subscription resource field reference (all verified at
# https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/reference/rest/v1/projects.subscriptions):
#
#   deadLetterPolicy.deadLetterTopic:    fully-qualified topic resource name
#                                        format: projects/PROJECT/topics/TOPIC
#   deadLetterPolicy.maxDeliveryAttempts: integer, valid range 5–100 (minimum 5;
#                                        values below 5 are rejected by the API)
#   retryPolicy.minimumBackoff:          duration string e.g. "10s" (max 600s)
#   retryPolicy.maximumBackoff:          duration string e.g. "600s" (max 600s)
#   pushConfig.pushEndpoint:             the Cloud Function TRANSFORM URL
#                                        (NOT the Cloud Run /call URL — see
#                                        §TWO-HOP TOPOLOGY above)
#   pushConfig.oidcToken.serviceAccountEmail: the push SA email
#   pushConfig.oidcToken.audience:       the TRANSFORM URL (no trailing slash)
#
# retryPolicy on push subscriptions is supported (verified: the Subscription
# resource schema applies retryPolicy to both push and pull subscriptions).
#
# The gcloud CLI uses different flag names from the REST API field names.
# CRITICAL: --push-endpoint is the TRANSFORM function URL, not /call. A direct
# push to /call returns HTTP 422 (raw envelope, work_item missing) on EVERY
# delivery → every message dead-lettered. The transform is mandatory.

# gcloud pubsub subscriptions create ${PUSH_SUB} \
#   --topic=${TOPIC} \
#   --project=${PROJECT_ID} \
#   \
#   --push-endpoint="${TRANSFORM_URL}" \
#   --push-auth-service-account="${PUSH_SA}" \
#   --push-auth-token-audience="${TRANSFORM_URL}" \
#   \
#   --ack-deadline=600 \
#   # ackDeadlineSeconds=600 (maximum); set >= your agent.call() expected runtime.
#   # See §ACK DEADLINE above for the 504→NACK→storm failure mode.
#   \
#   --dead-letter-topic=projects/${PROJECT_ID}/topics/${DLQ_TOPIC} \
#   --max-delivery-attempts=5 \
#   # maxDeliveryAttempts minimum is 5; values below 5 rejected by API.
#   # Range 5-100. After this many failed deliveries, the message is
#   # forwarded to the dead-letter topic and the DLQ alert fires.
#   \
#   --min-retry-delay=10s \
#   # retryPolicy.minimumBackoff; Duration string (e.g. "10s"). Range 0-600s.
#   --max-retry-delay=600s
#   # retryPolicy.maximumBackoff; Duration string (e.g. "600s"). Range 0-600s.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# See extras/gcp/README.md §Pub/Sub ingestion runbook for the full operator
# guide, transform snippet, idempotency contract, and troubleshooting.
# The DLQ depth monitoring alert is in extras/gcp/cloud-monitoring-policies.yaml
# (policy 5 — "DLQ depth > 0").
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
